/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A control volume is a chosen region of space.
   - ṁ is mass flow rate in kg/s; in and out mark streams entering and leaving the control volume.
   - A stream carries energy in three forms per kilogram: internal energy u, kinetic energy c²/2 and potential energy gz, where c is the stream speed in m/s (this unit writes speed as c so that v stays specific volume).
   - h is specific enthalpy in kJ/kg; u is specific internal energy in kJ/kg; P is pressure in kPa; v is specific volume in m³/kg, so Pv is in kJ/kg.
   - With Q positive into the control volume and Ws (shaft power) positive out of it, the steady-flow energy equation for any number of streams is:
   - Q is the heat rate into the control volume in kW; Ws is the shaft power delivered by it in kW; ṁ is mass flow rate in kg/s; c is speed in m/s; g = 9.81 m/s²; z is elevation in m.
   - For one inlet and one outlet with the same ṁ, divide by ṁ.
   - q and ws are in kJ/kg; h in kJ/kg; c in m/s (divide the c² term by 1000 for kJ/kg); z in m.
   - Neglect Δke or Δpe only after you estimate them.
   - When a tank fills or empties, the mass and energy inside change with time, so the storage term stays in.
*/
