/* book exam traps: Using u instead of h for a flowing stream: the flow work Pv is already in h. For a turbine or compressor, h is the property you look up in steam tables. | Dropping kinetic energy because it looks small: convert m²/s² to kJ/kg by dividing by 1000, then compare with Δh. 100 m/s gives 5 kJ/kg, not 5000 kJ/kg. | Sign slip on Q and Ws: Q is positive into the control volume; Ws is positive out. A 50 kW heat loss is Q = −50 kW, not +50 kW. | Treating flow work as shaft work: Pv is not Ws. Ws crosses the boundary through a shaft; Pv crosses with the fluid. | Applying the steady-flow equation to a tank that is filling or emptying. Then the mass and energy inside change, and the storage term d(mu)cv/dt stays in; for filling an empty insulated tank, u₂ = hline, not h₂ = hline. */
